    "q-122960": {
      "dom_id": "step-q122960",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Let $R=[0,3]\\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=6-x-y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{0,1,2,3\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{0,2\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14224902/assets/178108b256e55eaa5bd58c72f606fbed0aef774ff10a46e059c70abe93f8b341.png)\n\nThe lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{31}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{31})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=2$ for all $3$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=2{m}_{11}+2{m}_{21}+2{m}_{31}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=6-x-y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=6-x-y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14224902/assets/dd521e876abfc4313afb5beaf568c1d93cad670cfa912e210ed5d1548c51ec97.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14224902/assets/0515af2764a6ac27b2b69998ea4b99079dd7266a5989a161d01a6d25a670678e.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(1,2)=3 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,2)=2 \\\\ {m}_{31} & =f(3,2)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=2\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 1=12.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
