      "source_example_id": "e-6242",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Decreasing Function",
      "kp_id": "e-6242"
    },
    "q-75040": {
      "dom_id": "step-q75040",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Let $R=[0,2]\\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=x+y+10.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{0,1,2\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{0,1,2\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14224902/assets/3cac974f7352243bfbe008ad8af58ae8222709f81f434070383cc759a46747c7.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y+10$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y+10$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14224902/assets/d7e548ae50bc8c7f0e33e8bb43ba8950788f735af103c4923d50d147995a3bb6.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14224902/assets/c6bdb6a479878c3fed94923a5db19d5e55db3431b685344d54b9a384f70a60e3.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(0,0)=10 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(1,0)=11 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(0,1)=11 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(1,1)=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & =10+11+11+12 \\\\  & =44.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
